119:beyond merely elucidating sample heterogeneity. Deviation scores obtained from these models also can be used as inputs for downstream analyses. Instead of using the raw features (i.e. relative power, functional connectivity, etc.), group average, classi /uniFB01 cation, and prediction analyses can be run using the deviation scores inferred from the models that can serve as inputs. For instance, Rutherford et al. [26] found minor (regression) to strong (group difference testing) advantages of using deviation scores over raw features. We have indeed tested this in the current paper and showed that NM can indeed improve group-level comparison between HC and cases. As such, normative models can contribute to the development of more personalized electrophysiological approaches.
127:One potential limitation is that the population included in the study has a restricted age range (5 -18 years), encompassing only younger, developmental stages. This may contribute to the observed heterogeneity, potentially due to varying developmental trajectories. However, in our recent study involving healthy controls and patients with neurodegenerative diseases [41], we observed similar patterns of heterogeneity in a population aged over 40. We therefore speculate that, although age may moderate certain features, the observed heterogeneity is not solely attributable to age differences. A more comprehensive understanding could be achieved through lifespan models highlighting developmental and non-developmental phases.
129:Another challenge of the study stems from the inherent heterogeneity that may exist within each group of patients who present multiple psychiatric diagnoses. This comorbidity (Table S1) introduces a layer of complexity. The presence of comorbidities poses challenges in isolating the unique contributions of each condition. This complexity is re /uniFB02 ective of the clinical reality where patients frequently exhibit overlapping symptomatology, necessitating a comprehensive approach to diagnosis and treatment. We were aware of this key point when quantifying heterogeneity and we provided an additional control analysis by investigating heterogeneity among patients who have only one diagnosis (according to the HBN dataset). The overlap results of the functional connectivity were always low and did not exceed 30%. This con /uniFB01 rms that the observed heterogeneity is indeed intrinsic to the disorder and not driven by the possible comorbidity. In addition, we have controlled for some available parameters such as IQ (Fig. S12 -S14) and sex (Fig. S2 -S11, Table S2, S3).

